---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '@/data/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Work on WordPress with Admin Access — SiteRadian"
  description="Use SiteRadian for supported, governed WordPress operations when you have WordPress Admin access but no SSH, FTP, SFTP, or hosting-panel credentials."
>
  <header class="page-hero admin-use-case-hero">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">WordPress Admin-only workflow</p>
      <h1>Serious WordPress work with the access you already have.</h1>
      <p class="lede">When a client gives you WordPress Admin but no SSH or SFTP, SiteRadian provides a governed path for supported AI-assisted operations through WordPress itself.</p>
      <div class="button-row"><a class="button" href={withBase('/docs/getting-started/')}>Start with WordPress Admin</a><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/integrations/')}>Choose an assistant</a></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">The access gap</p><h2 class="content-heading">WordPress Admin may be all you receive.</h2><p>Freelancers, agencies, site owners, and support teams often need to work on a site without access to its shell, file transfer, hosting panel, or database server. Waiting for broader credentials can stall work that WordPress itself is already capable of supporting.</p><p>SiteRadian turns authorized WordPress access into a defined operations interface for compatible AI assistants. The assistant does not receive general server control; it receives the SiteRadian scope and capabilities you choose.</p></div><aside class="scenario-card"><p class="eyebrow">A practical scenario</p><h3>A client site. One wp-admin account. No infrastructure credentials.</h3><p>Install SiteRadian with an account that can manage plugins, create Read-only access, connect a supported assistant, and begin with a site inspection. Expand access only when a specific supported operation requires it.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="section soft"><div class="container"><div class="section-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">Alternative operational path</p><h2>Through WordPress. Within scope. Under governance.</h2><p>SiteRadian provides a bounded route to supported work; it does not imitate a shell or expose an unrestricted file system.</p></div></div><div class="admin-operation-grid"><article><span>01</span><h3>Use WordPress Admin</h3><p>Install and activate SiteRadian through the standard Plugins screen when your account has permission.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Connect with narrow access</h3><p>Create a SiteRadian credential, begin Read-only, and grant only the capabilities the work needs.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Govern each operation</h3><p>SiteRadian validates access, applies protection and approval, executes supported work, and records the outcome.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Verified operation categories</p><h2 class="content-heading">Useful reach inside WordPress.</h2><p>The current SiteRadian catalogue includes diagnostic reads and governed operations across content, media, users, settings, themes, plugins, history, and WooCommerce areas such as products, inventory, pricing, orders, customers, coupons, and variations.</p><p>Exact availability, required capability, risk, and rollback support depend on the operation. Start by asking the assistant to inspect the site without changing anything.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/docs/scoped-access/')}>Understand scoped access</a><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/docs/protection/')}>Review protection modes</a></div></div><div class="boundary-card"><p class="eyebrow">What stays outside</p><h3>Server work remains server work.</h3><ul><li>Operating-system administration</li><li>Hosting and network configuration</li><li>Infrastructure-level changes</li><li>Direct database or filesystem work outside supported operations</li><li>Recovery when WordPress itself is unavailable</li></ul><p class="boundary-footnote">These tasks may still require SSH, SFTP, a hosting control panel, database access, or provider-specific tooling.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="section soft"><div class="container"><div class="section-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">Who this helps</p><h2>Less credential sharing. More deliberate WordPress work.</h2></div></div><div class="audience-grid audience-grid-four"><article class="audience-card"><h3>Freelancers</h3><p>Start supported work when a client provides wp-admin but not server credentials.</p></article><article class="audience-card"><h3>Agencies</h3><p>Use repeatable, scoped access across client sites without broadly distributing infrastructure credentials.</p></article><article class="audience-card"><h3>Site owners</h3><p>Work with a familiar AI assistant without learning shell administration for supported WordPress tasks.</p></article><article class="audience-card"><h3>Support teams</h3><p>Diagnose and perform supported operations through a controlled path with approvals and history.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container availability"><div class="availability-inner"><div><p class="eyebrow">Start with least access</p><h2>Inspect first. Expand only when the task requires it.</h2><p>Use the standard WordPress installation path, create a Read-only credential, connect the exact assistant you use, and verify the site before requesting a change.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href={withBase('/docs/getting-started/')}>Follow Getting Started</a><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/security/')}>Read the security model</a><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/support/')}>Visit Support</a></div></div></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
